1	//! Engine-API consumer that advances the chain on a 2s cadence aligned to
2	//! wall-clock time, with **greedy backfill** of any timestamp slots that
3	//! were missed due to a reorg or downtime.
4	//!
5	//! [`Sequencer::run`] is the long-running loop. Each iteration handles one
6	//! of:
7	//!
8	//! 1. A scheduler tick. `Sequencer::advance` runs in a loop, producing
9	//!    blocks at deterministic `parent.timestamp() + BLOCK_TIME_SECS`
10	//!    slots until the chain catches up to the tick's wall-clock target.
11	//!    Most ticks produce a single block (the chain is on cadence); after
12	//!    a reorg or a stall, the same tick may produce several filler
13	//!    blocks to backfill the slots that passed during the rewind.
14	//! 2. A slower FCU-refresh timer, used to keep reth's engine view alive
15	//!    during quiet periods.
16	//!
17	//! The Sequencer does **not** keep a local mirror of the canonical head.
18	//! Every iteration of `advance` reads the current head from the
19	//! [`BlockCommitterHandle::last_header`] — the actor is the single source
20	//! of truth, updated synchronously inside every Sequence + Derive command.
21	//! This is what fixes the race where the Deriver-driven canonical change
22	//! was visible to the actor immediately, but the Sequencer's own mirror
23	//! lagged behind reth's `CanonStateNotification` broadcast — producing
24	//! "invalid payload attributes" errors when the Sequencer issued FCU+attrs
25	//! based on a stale parent.
